impact-type: compatibility-break
description: A backslash in a date template now escapes the next character of the template, where it used to be removed from the finished text.

Up to 5.4.1 the escapes were stripped from the rendered result rather than from the template, so a backslash consumed whatever the token after it had produced.

|!Template |!Up to 5.4.1 |!From 5.5.0 |
|`MM0\D\D` |`110DD` |`110DD` |
|`DDth MMM \M\M\M YYYY` |`9th November MMM 2014` |`9th November MMM 2014` |
|`\0MM` |`11` |`011` |
|`\MM` |`11` |`MM` |
|`\YYYY` |`2014` |`Y14Y` |

Both examples given in [[DateFormat]] are unchanged, because in those the escaped character is not one a token begins with. A template that escapes a character which does start a token, and relied on the token still being substituted, renders differently. Remove the backslash in that case.
